String vs StringBuilder vs StringBuffer Comparison
Code
String s = "abc";
String s1 = new String("abc");
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("abc");
StringBuffer sbf = new StringBuffer("abc");

System.out.println(s == s1);                  // false
System.out.println(s.equals(s1));             // true

System.out.println(s.equals(sb));             // false
System.out.println(s.equals(sb.toString()));  // true

System.out.println(s.equals(sbf));            // false
System.out.println(s.equals(sbf.toString())); // true
Output
false
true
false
true
false
true
Key Points
== → Compares references (memory addresses).
equals() → Compares contents (only with another String).
StringBuilder and StringBuffer are not String objects.
Use toString() to compare a StringBuilder or StringBuffer with a String.
Difference Between StringBuilder and StringBuffer
StringBuilder	StringBuffer
Not thread-safe	Thread-safe (synchronized)
Faster	Slightly slower
Used in single-threaded applications	Used in multi-threaded applications